INT. RAILWAY WAITING ROOM - NIGHT (1884)

A cold hall of benches and a cast-iron stove. Gaslight hisses. Snow has stopped the last train.

MRS. HARRIET COLLINGWOOD, sixties, in black bombazine, knits by the stove. A MAN in a worn greatcoat sits on the far bench, hat low, a carpetbag between his boots.

MAN
Good evening, ma'am.

HARRIET
It is not, sir. It is snowing.

MAN
So it is.

She knits. The needles click. He rubs his hands.

MAN (CONT'D)
Are you waiting for the Dover connection?

HARRIET
I am waiting for my nephew. He is meant to meet me.

MAN
In this?

HARRIET
He is a very unreliable young man.

The man laughs, a short warm sound.

HARRIET (CONT'D)
And you, sir? You have the look of a person who has been waiting longer than a night.

MAN
Twenty years, ma'am. My name is Elias Fairchild.

Harriet's needles stop. She looks up for the first time.

HARRIET
Fairchild. You are Charles's brother.

ELIAS
I am.

HARRIET
He spoke of you with great bitterness.

ELIAS
He had cause.

She resumes knitting. After a moment she pats the bench beside her. Elias crosses the hall and sits.
